---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { site, author, sections } from "../data/site";

const base = Astro.site?.toString() ?? site.url;
const jsonLd = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "AboutPage",
    name: `About ${site.name}`,
    url: new URL("/about/", base).toString(),
    mainEntity: {
      "@type": "Person",
      name: author.name,
      description: author.bio,
      ...(author.sameAs.length ? { sameAs: author.sameAs } : {}),
    },
  },
];
---
<Base title={`About | ${site.name}`} description={`Who writes ${site.name} and how the columns are researched.`} path="/about/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="article-page">
    <Header />
    <main class="static-main">
      <p class="static-eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Who writes this, and how</h1>

      <p>
        {site.name} publishes plain-English columns on U.S. federal tax rules. Each
        column answers one question, states the rule, and links the IRS publication
        or Internal Revenue Code section it comes from.
      </p>

      <h2>The author</h2>
      <p><strong>{author.name}</strong> — {author.role}</p>
      <p>{author.bio}</p>
      <p>
        <strong>What I am not:</strong> I am not a CPA, an enrolled agent or a tax
        attorney. I hold no U.S. tax credential of any kind, I do not prepare or
        review returns, and I cannot represent anyone before the IRS. Working at a
        tax firm in Korea gives me no standing in U.S. tax whatsoever — it is
        where I learned to read a rule at its source, and that is all it is.
        Nothing on this site is professional tax advice.
      </p>

      <h2>How a column gets written</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Start from one question a real filer would type into a search box.</li>
        <li>Find the rule in the primary source — an IRS publication, form instructions, the Internal Revenue Code or a Treasury regulation.</li>
        <li>Write the answer in plain English, with the numbers and deadlines that apply.</li>
        <li>List every source at the foot of the column so you can check it yourself.</li>
        <li>Re-check year-specific columns when the IRS publishes new figures, and stamp the review date on the page.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we cover</h2>
      <ul>
        {sections.map((s) => (
          <li><strong>{s.label}</strong> — {s.description} ({s.topics})</li>
        ))}
      </ul>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Tax rules change and mistakes happen. If a column is wrong or out of date,
        say so and it gets fixed — corrections are made on the page itself with an
        updated review date, not quietly deleted.
      </p>

      <h2>How this site makes money</h2>
      <p>
        Through display advertising, affiliate links to software, and a free
        newsletter. Affiliate links never change what a column says, and no product
        is recommended for paying us. See the
        <a href="/disclaimer/">editorial policy</a> for the full disclosure.
      </p>
    </main>
    <Footer />
  </div>
</Base>
